0216: present timing polls the GPU completion instead of blocking in WaitAny

With implicit device synchronization, Instance::WaitAny with a timeout holds Dawn's device lock
until the GPU finishes, so every other thread's Dawn call (the render worker encoding the next
frame, the FIFO thread creating textures, the game thread's EFB readbacks) waited for the GPU as
well. LM's original-30 path (no frame-ahead limit) fell from 28 to 18.5 fps at 4K with enhanced
lighting while the record was on (docs/INTERPOLATION.md "Pass 5"). The waiter thread now polls
with a zero timeout every ~0.25 ms; a done stamp is at most that late.

diff --git a/lib/aurora.cpp b/lib/aurora.cpp
--- a/lib/aurora.cpp
+++ b/lib/aurora.cpp
@@ -286,10 +286,20 @@
       p = g_pending.front();
     }
     bool ok = false;
-    for (int i = 0; i < 50 && !ok; i++) {  // up to 5 s
-      ok = webgpu::g_instance.WaitAny(p.future, 100'000'000) == wgpu::WaitStatus::Success;
+    // 0216: polled, never a blocking WaitAny. With implicit device synchronization a WaitAny with a
+    // timeout holds Dawn's device lock until the GPU finishes, so every other thread's Dawn call
+    // (render worker encoding the next frame, FIFO thread creating textures, the game thread's
+    // EFB readbacks) waited for the GPU too: the original-30 path (no frame-ahead limit) fell from
+    // 28 to 18.5 fps at 4K with this record on. A zero-timeout poll every ~0.25 ms takes the lock
+    // only briefly; the stamp is up to that late.
+    const auto deadline = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + std::chrono::seconds(5);
+    while (!ok && std::chrono::steady_clock::now() < deadline) {
+      ok = webgpu::g_instance.WaitAny(p.future, 0) == wgpu::WaitStatus::Success;
       if (g_stop) {
         return;
+      }
+      if (!ok) {
+        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::microseconds(250));
       }
     }
     p.t.doneNs = ok ? now_ns() : 0;
